A||||3||dans87gt||Heater hose replacement||||1
Z||000000||dans87gt||10-25-1999||07:20 AM||||   How or where can you get the heater hoses replaced? Both of my rear heater hoses need replaced, these hoses are factory clamped at one end. Can these be taken to an airconditioning shop, it looks like the clamps are the same as on the air-conditioning  hoses. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-25-1999||09:17 AM||||cut off the clamps with a small grinder or dremmel tool. if they are splitting, stick a screw driver in the crack and twist sometimes that will crack them right off.<P>then clean the tubing, replace the lines and clamp with standard screw drive clamps. then if they are ever damaged they are easily fixed on the road side. For the ones under the car you might even want to consider some stainless steel jacketing to protect them from road debris. use good clamps, the kind you can put a socket wrench on. then use a 1/4 inch drive ratchet to tighten them. you'll get them tight more easily without slipping off them 15 times or stripping the slot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dans87gt||10-25-1999||12:03 PM||||    Do I have to "flare" the end of the heater tubes?, I was concerned about clamp coming loose.                                               Why in the world did Pontiac do this???||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-25-1999||02:36 PM||||no you don't have to flair it. finding a "double flair" tool that big is a pain anyway. If you want a safety stop then one way you could do it is solder a turn of wire to the steel pipe. you just have to tack it in 3-4 places. There isn't any easy way on AL pipe.<P>Good clamps, tightened properly, won't come loose unless you want them to.<P>they do it because it's cheap and the machine that crimps them always does it exactly the same way. makes it more reliable untill the AL sleave rots out. Generally the crimp out lasts the car.||||reg||1
